{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded receipt bd1e9e66ffbc4ab58902e32fdb7a0096 in the failed-configuration-write account. The phantom commit reproduced in the grown direction: the AddVoter write failed while the latest configuration grew to three voters at index 3 over a log ending at 2, the leader kept office through the phantom quorum, and a client command committed at index 4 while the committed peer's log ended at 2. After that leader was shut down and restarted over its own retained store, it loaded only A and B with two voters, the new server was absent, the last stored index was still 4 and the stored commit index read 0. The account therefore records that the phantom configuration is confined to the process that installed it and that the entry committed under it survives as an ordinary stored entry whose commitment is no longer recorded, leaving its fate to the committed pair. Two limitations were added: one stating that a single restart with the peer still partitioned and no snapshot does not cover later restarts or a second failed change, and one recording that this request's comparison was evaluated as unknown because its prerequisite event is labeled phantom_commit while the result is labeled phantom_restart, so the finding rests on the recorded events and later requests should use a single case label. The counterevidence entry about restart durability now covers both directions, and both phantom knowledge entries gain the receipt.\"}"}}
